C++ supports **object-oriented programming**. A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance.

Basic class:

```cpp
class Player {
public:
    std::string name;
    int hp;

    void takeDamage(int amount) {
        hp -= amount;
    }
};
```

Note the **semicolon after the closing brace** — easy to forget and a common compile error.

`public:` makes members accessible from outside the class. Members before `private:` (the default) can only be used inside the class.

Create and use objects:

```cpp
Player hero;
hero.name = "Aria";
hero.hp = 100;
hero.takeDamage(25);
std::cout << hero.hp << std::endl;   // 75
```

A **constructor** runs on creation:

```cpp
class Player {
public:
    std::string name;
    int hp;

    Player(std::string n, int h) {
        name = n;
        hp = h;
    }
};

Player hero("Aria", 100);   // constructor call
```

